export const FIGURE_ITEMS_C46 = [
  {"id":"U4-D19","topic":"u4-respiration","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.2/LO4","fig":"P-U4-01","target":"arrow-o2","q":"Tap the arrow that shows how oxygen moves at this gill surface.","why":"Oxygen dissolved in the water crosses the thin gill surface into the blood, so its arrow points from the water down into the capillary, where the blood cells are. The arrow pointing the other way, from the blood up into the water, is carbon dioxide leaving the blood. The arrow above the surface shows the water flowing over the gill; it does not cross into the blood."},
  {"id":"U4-D20","topic":"u4-respiration","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.2/LO4","fig":"P-U4-01","q":"The gill has a large surface area, thin walls and a good blood supply. Which process do these features make efficient?","options":["Gas exchange: oxygen passes into the blood and carbon dioxide passes out","Respiration: the gill cells break down glucose to release energy for the fish","Digestion: the gills break food down into glucose that dissolves in the blood","Respiration: the gills use the oxygen in the water to make glucose for the fish"],"a":0,"why":"The gill is a gas-exchange surface. A large area, a thin barrier and a good blood supply let oxygen move into the blood and carbon dioxide move out quickly. Respiration is a different process: the chemical reactions in every cell that release energy from glucose. It uses the oxygen that gas exchange supplies. Gills do not digest food, and no part of a fish makes glucose."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U4-81","topic":"u4-respiration","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.2/LO4","fig":"P-U4-01","target":"gill-surface","q":"Oxygen gets from the water into the blood quickly because it has only a very short distance to travel. Tap the feature that keeps this distance short.","why":"The single thin layer of flat cells between the water and the blood is the gas-exchange surface. Because it is so thin, oxygen and carbon dioxide cross it quickly. The water flowing over the gill and the blood in the capillary also help gas exchange, by bringing fresh water and blood, but they are not the barrier the gases cross. The two arrows show the gases moving, not a part of the gill."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U4-82","topic":"u4-respiration","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.2/LO4","fig":"P-U4-01","q":"The water flowing over this gill now holds very little dissolved oxygen. What happens to the fish?","options":["Less oxygen enters its blood, so its cells release less energy by respiration","Its gill cells respire for the whole body, so its other cells are not affected","More carbon dioxide enters its blood from the water to replace the oxygen","It makes more glucose in its gills, so its cells need less oxygen for energy"],"a":0,"why":"Gas exchange at the gill supplies the oxygen that every cell needs for aerobic respiration. With little oxygen in the water, less crosses into the blood, so the cells release less energy and the fish becomes less active and may die. The gills do not respire for the rest of the body: every cell respires. Carbon dioxide still moves out of the blood into the water, and a fish cannot make glucose."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U4-83","topic":"u4-photosynthesis","type":"tap","tier":2,"ref":"4.3/LO8","fig":"P-U4-04","target":"upwelling-coast","q":"The arrows on the land show the direction of the wind. Tap the area where productivity is high because of upwelling.","why":"Where the wind blows surface water away from the coast, cold water from the deep rises to replace it. This upwelled water is rich in nutrients released by decay in the deep, so producers in the sunlit surface photosynthesise rapidly. The other high area, at the river mouth, gets its nutrients from run-off, not upwelling. The open ocean far from the coast gets few nutrients from either source, so its productivity is low."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U5-81","topic":"u5-components","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"5.1/LO5","fig":"P-U4-04","q":"On this map, productivity is high in the water near the river mouth. Why?","options":["Run-off from the land carries nutrients that producers use to grow","The river lowers the salinity, and producers grow faster in fresh water","The river makes the water cloudy, which helps producers absorb light","River water is warmer, so it holds more oxygen for the producers"],"a":0,"why":"Rivers carry run-off from the land into the sea, and the run-off contains dissolved nutrients such as nitrates. Producers take these up and grow faster, so coastal water near a river mouth is highly productive. Salinity is not what drives productivity, cloudy water lets less light through to the producers, and warm water holds less dissolved oxygen, not more."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U4-84","topic":"u4-photosynthesis","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.3/LO8","fig":"P-U4-04","q":"For several months, the winds shown on the map stop blowing. What is most likely to happen to productivity along that stretch of coast?","options":["It falls, because less nutrient-rich deep water rises to the surface","It rises, because the surface water warms and holds more oxygen","It stays high, because the river still brings nutrients to that coast","It falls, because the wind no longer blows nutrients off the land"],"a":0,"why":"The wind blowing off the coast drives the upwelling. When it stops, less cold, nutrient-rich water rises into the sunlit surface, so the producers have fewer nutrients and productivity falls, as in an El Niño year. The river mouth is further up the coast, so its run-off does not feed this stretch. Warmer water holds less oxygen, and the wind does not carry the nutrients itself: it moves the surface water away."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U5-82","topic":"u5-investigating","type":"label","tier":1,"ref":"5.2/LO2","fig":"P-U5-04","extra":["random quadrats","shore profile"],"q":"Both strips show the same rocky shore, from the top of the shore (left) to the sea (right). Label the sampling method shown in each strip.","why":"A line transect records the organisms touching the tape at set intervals along it (the rings). A belt transect places a quadrat at set intervals along the tape and records what is inside each one. Neither uses random positions: both are systematic. A shore profile measures the slope of the shore, not the organisms."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U5-83","topic":"u5-investigating","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"5.2/LO2","fig":"P-U5-04","q":"What will the results of the method in the lower strip show?","options":["How the numbers of each organism change down the shore, from top to sea","The total number of each kind of organism living on the whole of this shore","Which organisms touch the tape at each point, but not how many there are","A random sample of the shore, free from bias in where the frames are placed"],"a":0,"why":"A belt transect counts the organisms in quadrats placed at set intervals along a line down the shore, so it shows how their distribution changes from the top of the shore to the sea. It does not give the total population, because only small parts of the shore are counted. Recording only what touches the tape is a line transect, and these quadrats are placed systematically, not at random."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U5-84","topic":"u5-investigating","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"5.2/LO2","fig":"P-U5-04","q":"In the lower strip, the quadrats are placed at set intervals along the tape, not at random. Why?","options":["Set intervals sample each part of the shore in turn, so changes show up","Random positions would show the distribution better, but take much longer","Set intervals give the total population of each organism on the shore","Set intervals make sure that each quadrat contains a different species"],"a":0,"why":"A transect is systematic sampling: quadrats at set intervals sample each part of the shore in turn, from the top to the sea, so the change in distribution along that line can be seen. Random quadrats are used to estimate population size or species richness over an area; they do not show change along a line. A transect does not give a total population, and the interval has nothing to do with which species a quadrat contains."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U6-81","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO5","fig":"P-U6-08","target":"medium-mesh","q":"The nets and the fish are all drawn at the same scale. A fisher wants a net that still catches the adult fish but lets the young fish escape. Tap that net.","why":"A fish escapes if its body fits through one hole of the mesh. On the middle net the young fish fits inside one hole, but the adult does not, so the adult is caught and the young fish can swim free to grow and breed. The small mesh holds both fish, so young fish are caught too. The large mesh lets the adult through as well, so the fisher would catch nothing of this size."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U6-82","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.3/LO5","fig":"P-U6-08","q":"A law makes fishers change from the left-hand net to the middle net. How does this help keep the fish stock sustainable?","options":["More young fish survive to breed, so the fish caught are replaced","The adult fish escape through the larger holes and breed instead","Fewer fish are caught in total, so each fisher earns more from the catch","Young fish are caught and sold, so fewer adults need to be caught"],"a":0,"why":"A larger minimum mesh size lets young fish escape. They survive to grow and breed, so the stock replaces the fish that are caught and stays stable. The middle net still holds the adult fish, so the adults do not escape. The law protects the young; it is not about income, and the middle net catches fewer young fish, not more."},
  {"id":"U6-D04","topic":"u6-plastic","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.6/LO2","fig":"P-U6-14","target":"b-predator","q":"Tap the organism that has the most microplastic in its body.","why":"Microplastic is not digested or broken down, so it stays in the body. Each predator eats many prey over its life and takes in all the pieces they contained, so the plastic builds up along the food chain. The top predator ends up with the most; the phytoplankton at the bottom have the least."},
  {"id":"U6-D05","topic":"u6-plastic","type":"label","tier":1,"ref":"6.6/LO2 · 4.4/LO7","fig":"P-U6-14","extra":["decomposer","detritivore"],"q":"Label the feeding role of each organism, from producer up to top predator.","why":"The arrows point from prey to predator, the direction energy flows. The phytoplankton are the producers; the small fish eats them, so it is the primary consumer; the larger fish eats the small fish, so it is the secondary consumer; and the shark eats the larger fish, so it is the tertiary consumer and the top predator. Nothing here breaks down or eats dead material, so no organism is a decomposer or a detritivore."},
  {"id":"U6-D06","topic":"u6-plastic","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.6/LO2","fig":"P-U6-14","q":"What does the figure show about microplastic in a marine food chain?","options":["It is passed from prey to predator and builds up, so the top predator has the most.","It is digested and broken down at each step, so less of it remains higher up the chain.","The producers at the bottom take it in, but the animals above them stay free of it.","It makes each organism grow larger, which is why the predators higher up are bigger."],"a":0,"why":"Plankton take in microplastic, and it is not broken down, so it passes to each predator when it eats its prey and builds up along the food chain. The number of pieces rises at every step, so it is not digested away, and every animal in the chain contains some. The predators are bigger because of what they are, not because of the plastic."},
  {"id":"U6-D01","topic":"u6-eutrophication","type":"label","tier":1,"ref":"6.7/LO5","fig":"P-U6-17","extra":["oxygen released by the dead algae","salt washed off the land"],"q":"Label each stage of eutrophication shown, from the source of the nutrients to the death of the fish.","why":"Fertiliser spread on a field dissolves in rain and runs off into the sea. The extra nutrients cause an algal bloom at the surface. When the algae die they sink, and decomposer bacteria break them down, using up dissolved oxygen by aerobic respiration, so fish die. Dead algae use up oxygen rather than releasing it, and eutrophication is caused by nutrients, not salt."},
  {"id":"U6-D02","topic":"u6-eutrophication","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.7/LO5","fig":"P-U6-17","target":"e-decomp","q":"Tap the stage in the figure where dissolved oxygen is being removed from the water.","why":"Decomposer bacteria break down the dead algae on the seabed and respire aerobically, using up the dissolved oxygen faster than it is replaced, so the fish suffocate. The living bloom at the surface releases oxygen by photosynthesis, and the run-off and fertiliser add nutrients, not an oxygen demand of their own. The dead fish are the result, not the cause."},
  {"id":"U6-D03","topic":"u6-eutrophication","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.7/LO5","fig":"P-U6-17","q":"The fish on the seabed has died. What is the immediate cause of its death?","options":["Decomposers used up the oxygen breaking down the dead algae, so it could not respire.","The thick algal bloom blocked the light, so the fish could not find its food and starved.","The fertiliser in the run-off was toxic, so it poisoned the fish directly as it spread.","The run-off raised the salinity of the water beyond what the fish could tolerate."],"a":0,"why":"Dead algae are decomposed by bacteria whose aerobic respiration removes dissolved oxygen faster than it is replaced, so the fish cannot respire and suffocates. The bloom can shade the water, but that is not what kills the fish. Fertiliser nutrients are not a poison to fish, and run-off is fresh water, so it does not raise the salinity."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U6-83","topic":"u6-eutrophication","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.7/LO5","fig":"P-U6-17","q":"While they are alive, the algae in the bloom at the surface release oxygen by photosynthesis. Why does the dissolved oxygen in this water still fall?","options":["Decomposers use oxygen breaking down the dead algae faster than it is replaced","The living algae use up oxygen when they photosynthesise in bright sunlight","The fertiliser in the run-off reacts with the dissolved oxygen and removes it","The bloom warms the water, and the warm water is then unable to hold any oxygen"],"a":0,"why":"Living algae add oxygen, but the bloom does not last. When the algae die and sink, decomposer bacteria break them down and respire aerobically, using oxygen faster than photosynthesis and the air can replace it. Photosynthesis releases oxygen rather than using it, fertiliser does not react with dissolved oxygen, and the bloom does not heat the water enough to drive the oxygen out."},
  {"id":"U6-D10","topic":"u6-energy","type":"label","tier":1,"ref":"6.5/LO6","fig":"P-U6-11","extra":["oil being burned on the surface","oil droplets rising to the surface"],"q":"Label each part of the oil-spill response shown in the figure.","why":"The boom is a floating barrier with a skirt below it that holds the slick back from the shore. The skimmer floats in the slick and lifts oil off the surface into the boat. The aircraft sprays dispersant, which breaks the oil into tiny droplets that mix down into the water, not up to the surface. No oil is being burned here."},
  {"id":"U6-D11","topic":"u6-energy","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.5/LO6","fig":"P-U6-11","target":"o-dispersant","q":"Tap the method that clears oil from the surface but sends it down into the water, where it can harm fish and coral.","why":"Dispersant sprayed from the aircraft breaks the slick into tiny droplets that mix down into the water. This protects the surface and the shore, but the droplets are toxic to fish and coral, so near a reef it can do more harm than good. The skimmer lifts the oil out of the water, and the boom only holds the slick in place."},
  {"id":"U6-D12","topic":"u6-energy","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.5/LO6","fig":"P-U6-11","q":"What is the job of the floating barrier shown in the figure?","options":["It stops the slick spreading, for example towards the shore.","It soaks up and absorbs the oil from the water.","It lifts the oil from the water surface so it can be collected.","It breaks the oil into tiny droplets that mix into the water."],"a":0,"why":"A boom is a floating barrier that contains the slick and stops it spreading, here towards the shore. It is usually put out first, so the oil stays in one place for the skimmer. The skimmer lifts the oil off the surface, and dispersant breaks it into droplets."},
  {"id":"PQ2-U6-84","topic":"u6-energy","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.5/LO6","fig":"P-U6-11","q":"This spill is drifting towards a coral reef. Why might the method used from the aircraft do more harm than good there?","options":["Its droplets sink into the water, where they are toxic to fish and coral","It makes the oil evaporate, and the fumes then settle on the reef just below","It turns the oil solid, so the lumps sink and bury the coral beneath them","It holds the slick in one place above the reef for a much longer time"],"a":0,"why":"Dispersant breaks the oil into tiny droplets that mix down into the water instead of staying at the surface. Near a reef, these toxic droplets reach the fish and the coral. Dispersants do not make oil evaporate or turn solid, and holding the slick in one place is what a boom does, not a dispersant."}
];
